# Grain Regrowth and Bifacial Passivation for High‐Efficiency Wide‐Bandgap Perovskite Solar Cells

## abstract
The wide‐bandgap perovskite solar cell is a crucial part of perovskite/silicon tandem solar cells, which offer an avenue for surpassing the power conversion efficiency (PCE) limit of single‐junction silicon solar cells. However, the actual efficiency of such tandem solar cells today is diminished by the nonradiative recombination losses in the wide‐bandgap perovskite subcells. Here, this work reports a grain regrowth and bifacial passivation (GRBP) strategy to reduce recombination losses at the grain boundaries and perovskite/charge transport layer interfaces simultaneously. This is achieved by a posttreatment of perovskite films with a mixture of methylammonium thiocyanate (MASCN) and phenethylammonium iodide (PEAI). The MASCN induces the regrowth of perovskite grains and simultaneously facilitates the penetration of PEAI into the hole‐transport‐layer (HTL)/perovskite bottom interface. Thereby, the bulk and interface nonradiative recombination losses are reduced and the open‐circuit voltage in solar cells is considerably increased. PCEs of 21.9% and 19.9% for the 1.65‐eV bandgap opaque and semitransparent perovskite solar cells, respectively, are obtained. The encapsulated semitransparent perovskite solar cells retain their initial efficiency following 500 h of operation under one‐sun illumination in ambient conditions. The perovskite/silicon 4‐terminal (4‐T) tandem cells are fabricated with impressive PCEs 29.8% and 28.5% for 0.049 cm2 and 1 cm2 devices, respectively.

The wide-bandgap perovskite solar cell is a crucial part of perovskite/silicon tandem solar cells, which offer an avenue for surpassing the power conversion efficiency (PCE) limit of single-junction silicon solar cells. However, the actual efficiency of such tandem solar cells today is diminished by the nonradiative recombination losses in the wide-bandgap perovskite subcells. Here, this work reports a grain regrowth and bifacial passivation (GRBP) strategy to reduce recombination losses at the grain boundaries and perovskite/charge transport layer interfaces simultaneously. This is achieved by a posttreatment of perovskite films with a mixture of methylammonium thiocyanate (MASCN) and phenethylammonium iodide (PEAI). The MASCN induces the regrowth of perovskite grains and simultaneously facilitates the penetration of PEAI into the hole-transport-layer (HTL)/perovskite bottom interface. Thereby, the bulk and interface nonradiative recombination losses are reduced and the opencircuit voltage in solar cells is considerably increased. PCEs of $21.9\%$ and $19.9\%$ for the 1.65-eV bandgap opaque and semitransparent perovskite solar cells, respectively, are obtained. The encapsulated semitransparent perovskite solar cells retain their initial efficiency following $500h$ of operation under one-sun illumination in ambient conditions. The perovskite/silicon 4-terminal (4-T) tandem cells are fabricated with impressive PCEs $29.8\%$ and $28.5\%$ for $0.049\ c m^{2}$ and 1 $\mathsf{c m}^{2}$ devices, respectively.
## 1. Introduction
Increasing the power conversion efficiency (PCE) of solar cells is a crucial driving force for reducing the cost of photovoltaic electricity.[1,2] Recently, as the dominant technology in photovoltaics, the silicon solar cells have reached a record efficiency of $26.7\%$ , which is close to their practical limit of ${\approx}27\%$ ,[3] leaving little room to further improve their PCEs. Tandem solar cells, which use photon energy more efficiently, can overcome the PCE limit of single-junction cells. Perovskite/silicon tandem solar cells are promising for practical applications due to their high efficiency and low cost.[4] As top cells for tandem solar cells, the semitransparent wide-bandgap (bandgap $>$ $1.65\mathrm{eV}$ ) perovskite solar cells (PSCs), typically with an inverted p-i-n device structure, play an essential role in harvesting the high-energy photons and providing high PCEs for both two-terminal (2-T) and mechanically stacked four-terminal (4-T) tandem solar cells.[5,6] However, the wide-bandgap (WBG) perovskite usually suffer from large open circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{OC}})$ loss relative to their theoretical limit.[7,8] Many studies have shown that nonradiative recombination within the perovskite absorber and interfacial nonradiative recombination at the perovskite/charge extraction layer are mainly causes for $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ loss in WBG perovskites.[8–11] So, improving $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of devices without sacrificing short current density $(J_{\mathrm{SC}})$ and fill factor (FF) is vital for fabricating efficient wide-bandgap perovskite solar cells (WBG PSCs).
The nonradiative recombination losses at the grain boundaries and the interfacial contacts lead to large open circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{OC}})$ deficits in WBG PSCs,[9] since the grain boundaries and surfaces (on both sides) in perovskite films are prone to have much higher trap densities than that inside the grain.[12] To address this limitation, large organic ligands, such as phenylethylammonium $(\mathrm{PEA^{+}})$ , butylammonium, and guanidinium, are widely applied for surface passivation to improve device performance by forming two-dimensional (2D) perovskites on the top film surface.[13,14] Since the large organic cations are easily soluble in precursor solution, it is challenging to form a 2D layer precisely at the buried bottom interface by pre-introducing the large organic cations on the substrate. Therefore, the large organic ligands are typically used as additives in the precursor solution or for surface passivation.[13,15–17]
In previous reports, the FF of PSCs was limited to around or below $80\%$ when large organic ligands were introduced as additives in the precursors.[7–13] Although many efforts are devoted to further optimizing 2D organic ligands and improving charge transport in 2D perovskites,[18–20] the FF is still limited ${\approx}81\%$ .[21] The relatively low FF is mainly attributed to the poor charge transport in 2D perovskites with disordered arrangement.[8] An alternative strategy involves introducing a 2D passivation layer with optimized energy level via posttreatment on the perovskite surface and reducing the $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ loss between perovskite surface/ charge transport layer, especially for perovskite $/\mathrm{C}_{60}$ interface in p-i-n structure.[14,22,23] The 2D posttreatment, however, only acts on the top surface of the perovskite film and is unable to penetrate throughout the perovskite film and to reach the buried bottom interface. Therefore, it is still challenging to simultaneously passivate defects on both sides of perovskite film and at grain boundaries in PSCs.
In this work, we devise a grain regrowth and bifacial passivation (GRBP) strategy to reduce recombination losses at the grain boundaries and perovskite/charge transport layer interfaces simultaneously. We achieve this by a posttreatment of perovskite films with a mixture of methylammonium thiocyanate (MASCN) and phenethylammonium iodide (PEAI). The MASCN additive induces the regrowth of perovskite grains and simultaneously facilitates the penetration of PEAI into the buried bottom interface. Based on this GRBP strategy, the $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ losses at both perovskite/ETL and hole-transport-layer $(\mathrm{HTL})/$ perovskite interfaces are effectively mitigated. We obtain a champion PCE of $21.9\%$ $\mathrm{'}V_{\mathrm{OC}}=1.221\ \mathrm{V},$ $J_{\mathrm{SC}}=21.5~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , and $\mathrm{FF}=83.3\%)$ for opaque wide-bandgap PSCs and of $19.9\%$ $'_{\mathrm{OC}}=1.189$ V, $J_{\mathrm{SC}}=20.3\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , and $\mathrm{FF}=82.0\%\$ ) for semitransparent devices. The semitransparent wide-bandgap PSCs exhibit promising stability under continuous illumination, maintaining their initial PCE after $500\mathrm{~h~}$ of illumination. We further achieve a stabilized PCE of $29.8\%$ for 4-T tandem devices by mechanically integrating the semitransparent perovskite cells with the silicon heterojunction (SHJ) solar cells.
## 2. Results and Discussion
In this work, the 1.65-eV WBG perovskite films, with a composition of $\mathrm{FA_{0.8}C s_{0.15}M A_{0.05}P b(I_{0.82}B r_{0.18})_{3}}$ , were prepared using the one-step antisolvent assisted deposition method. As shown in Figure 1a, two posttreatment strategies were applied to improve the film quality by spin-coating the (1) PEAI solution or the (2) MASCN and PEAI mixed solution in isopropanol (IPA) onto perovskite films, followed by annealing at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 5 min. The resultant films are denoted as PEAI and GRBP, respectively. The pristine perovskite films without any posttreatment is denoted as control. The surface and cross-sectional scanning electron microscope (SEM) images of the three different perovskite films are compared in Figure  1b–d. For control and PEAI films, there are large bright features randomly distributed on the surface. In SEM images, the brighter features usually originate from regions with higher electron density.[24] Lead halides generally possess higher electron density than perovskite due to the absence of organic ligands. The bright features on the film surface are most likely attributed to the $\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{X}}_{2}$ species (X is a mixture of I and Br). For the GRBP films, the $\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{X}}_{2}$ related bright features are smaller in size and are uniformly distributed at the GBs. Meanwhile, the posttreatment with mixed MASCN and PEAI induced the regrowth of perovskite grains and thereby enlarged the grain size. Figure S1 (Supporting Information) shows the grain size distributions of the control, PEAI, and GRBP perovskite films. The control samples exhibited average grain size of $300~\mathrm{nm}$ , but the grain size become smaller after PEAI process. For comparison, the GRBP sample showed an enlarged average grain size up to $800\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The cross-section of the control and PEAI films showed stacked crystals with obvious GBs at the horizontal direction. For the GRBP case, the film had much fewer GBs with crystal grain throughout the entire film. Further investigations revealed that MASCN additive is responsible for the increase in grain size and crystal orientation (Figure S2, Supporting Information). The mixture of MASCN and PEAI synergistically improves the crystallinity of the film as will be further discussed in the following contents. The enlarged grain size effectively reduces the density of GBs. The grain regrowth also eliminate the horizontal GBs within the perovskite layer, which facilitates the charge transport throughout the absorber.[25]
We then performed grazing incidence X-ray diffraction (GIXRD) characterizations to gain the crystallographic information of perovskite films. The X-ray incident angles of $0.05^{\circ}$ and $1^{\circ}$ were applied to probe the near-surface and full-depth of the films, respectively.[26] Figure 2a–c and Figure S3 (Supporting Information) present the 2D GI-XRD patterns of the control, PEAI, and GRBP perovskite films with the X-ray incident angles of $1^{\circ}$ and $0.05^{\circ}$ , respectively. All the GI-XRD patterns exhibited diffraction rings corresponding to the $\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{X}}_{2}$ (at wavevector transfer $q_{\mathrm{z}}=0.9\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{-1}\$ and perovskite (at $q_{\mathrm{z}}=1.00$ , 1.41, 1.73, and $2.00\mathring\mathrm{A}^{-1}\big)$ . The GRBP perovskite film showed additional diffraction rings at $q=0.31\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{-1}$ , which is corresponding to the (020) peak of $n=2$ 2D perovskite.[27] The integrated 1D-XRD patterns of the different films detected with the X-ray incident angles of $0.05^{\circ}$ and $1^{\circ}$ are shown in Figure  1g,h, respectively. The peak area ratio between the $\mathsf{P b X}_{2}$ at $12.7^{\circ}$ and the peak for 3D (001) at $14.1^{\circ}$ decreases with the increase of the incident angle, indicating that the $\mathsf{P b X}_{2}$ phases are mainly located at the top surface of the perovskite films. Moreover, the GRBP sample possesses the lowest peak area ratio of $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{X}_{2}/3\mathrm{D}$ perovskite, since the formation of 2D perovskite consumes $\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{X}}_{2}$ . This phenomenon is consistent with the SEM results that the GRBP film possesses smaller $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{X}_{2}$ -related crystals around the GBs. For PEAI samples, there are no obvious signals of 2D perovskites on XRD patterns probably due to the low content of species below detectable limit. By increasing the concentration of PEAI, a new diffraction peak at $2\theta=10^{\circ}$ was observed (Figure S4, Supporting Information), which is ascribed to the PEAI crystals. Those results suggest that the PEAI posttreatment cannot form 2D perovskites on the wide-bandgap perovskite film surface. We conducted X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) measurements to confirm the existence of PEAI on perovskite in Figure S5 (Supporting Information). For the PEAI sample, in addition to $\mathsf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{C}$ , $\mathrm{C-N}$ , $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ peaks located at 284.2, 286.2, and $288.0\ \mathrm{eV},$ respectively. We also found that a peak emerges at a binding energy of $292.0\ \mathrm{eV}$ for PEAI sample, consistent with $\pi{-}\pi$ bonding in the phenyl functional group of the $\mathrm{PEA^{+}}$ [28] We consider that the $\mathrm{PEA}^{+}$ properly exist on the perovskite in PEAI molecular form.
We then performed X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) characterization to investigate whether the $\mathrm{{SCN^{-}}}$ ions still existed in GRBP perovskite films. Figure S6 (Supporting Information) shows the XPS spectra of perovskite film that was treated by the PEAI and MASCN mixed solution before annealing, suggesting the existence of SCN on the perovskite surface before annealing. The observed peak around $162\mathrm{eV}$ could be attributed to the $\mathrm{~S~}2\mathrm{p}$ orbits of the $\mathrm{{SCN^{-}}}$ ion.[29] Figure  2f shows the XPS spectra of perovskites after annealing process. The S signals were absent for the GRBP film after annealing, indicating the volatilization of $\mathrm{{SCN^{-}}}$ during the annealing process. This illustrates the main effect of $\mathrm{{SCN^{-}}}$ is assisting in the grain regrowth rather than doping in perovskite film.
As previously reported, the organic ammonium salt or the 2D perovskite passivation layer could effectively passivate defects in the perovskite films.[30] We subsequently deployed the different perovskite films to fabricate opaque PSCs. The opaque PSC devices adopt the p-i-n structure of glass/indium tin oxide (ITO)/SAM/perovskite $\ensuremath{I_{\mathrm{C}}}_{60}/\mathrm{BCP/Cu}$ , as shown in Figure 3a. The optoelectronic properties of devices with different PEAI and MASCN concentration are shown in Figure S7 (Supporting Information), and the optimal PEAI and GRBP solution are studied hereafter. The main photovoltaic parameters for the devices with different posttreatments are presented in Figure 3b. The GRBP devices exhibited improvement among all the PV parameters. The average $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ increases from $1.14\mathrm{V}$ for the control devices, to $1.19\mathrm{~V~}$ for the PEAI devices and further to $1.22{\mathrm{~V~}}$ for the GRBP-devices. Moreover, the GRBP devices showed a considerably improved average FF of $82.5\%$ compared to the control devices $(80.8\%)$ , while the PEAI devices had lowest value of $80.3\%$ . The $J{-}V$ and external quantum efficiency (EQE) curves of the champion devices are compared in Figure S8 (Supporting Information). The champion GRBP device exhibited a PCE of $21.9\%$ (with $V_{\mathrm{OC}}=1.221\ \mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{t}}$ $J_{\mathrm{SC}}=21.5~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , and $\mathrm{FF}=83.3\%)$ , which is substantially higher than the PEAI device (PCE of $20.6\%$ with $V_{\mathrm{OC}}=1.196\mathrm{~V~}$ , $J_{\mathrm{SC}}=21.4~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , and $\mathrm{FF}\ =\ 80.8\%$ and control device (PCE of $20.2\%$ with $V_{\mathrm{OC}}=1.148\ \mathrm{V},$ $J_{\mathrm{SC}}=21.6~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , and $\mathrm{FF}=80.6\%$ . The EQE of perovskite devices with different posttreatment process is shown in Figure S8b (Supporting Information). The absorption edges of different perovskites are merely the same, which illustrates that the bandgaps of perovskite have not changed. So, the difference of $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ and FF of the final devices is not affected by the band gap change. The champion GRBP device had very minor hysteresis $(21.9\%$ for the reverse scan and $21.8\%$ for the forward scan, Figure 3c), and exhibited a stabilized PCE of $21.8\%$ (Figure 3d). The integrated $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ values from EQE spectra are $21.5\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ for the GRBP device (Figure 3e), which is in good agreement with the $J{-}V$ characterization.
The improved PCE in GRBP devices PSCs is mainly attributed to the enhanced $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ . The $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of solar cells is related to the nonradiative recombination losses in the bulk and at the perovskite/charge transport layers interfaces. We then carried out photoluminescence quantum yield (PLQY) measurements to quantify the quasi-Fermi level splitting (QFLS) in the neat perovskite absorber layers and the multilayer stacks.[31,32] The detailed measurement methods are discussed in Note S1 (Supporting Information). Here, we measured three sets of layer stacks for control, PEAI and GRBP samples to investigate the nonradiative recombination in the bulk and interfaces, namely, (1) neat perovskite film, (2) HTL/perovskite, and (3) HTL/perovskite/ETL (structures shown in Figure S9, Supporting Information). The corresponding QFLS and nonradiative recombination losses are calculated and summarized in Figure 4a,b and Table S1 (Supporting Information). For a solar cell with a bandgap of $1.65\ \mathrm{eV},$ the $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ limit (same as $\mathrm{QFLS_{rad,}}$ ) is calculated to be $1373\mathrm{mV}.$ [33] For the neat perovskite films, the QFLS of the control film is $1321~\mathrm{mV}_{\mathrm{{i}}}$ which is slightly lower than those of the PEAI film $(1327\mathrm{mV}_{\perp}$ ) and GRBP film $(1332\mathrm{mV})$ . The minor differences in QFLS among the three types of samples suggest that bulk recombination is not the dominant factor that limits the device performance. By introducing the HTL, the QFLS values of the HTL/perovskite stacks are 1280, 1281, and $1307~\mathrm{mV}$ for the control, PEAI, and GRBP cases, respectively. The corresponding estimated voltage losses at the HTL/perovskite interfaces are 41, 46, and $25~\mathrm{mV},$ respectively. The GRBP sample shows obviously higher QFLS than the other samples. Notably, defect passivation at the bottom “buried interface” is achieved after the posttreatment of mixed MASCN and PEAI. Furthermore, the QFLS values of the HTL/perovskite/ETL stacks are 1166, 1191, and $1229\ensuremath{\mathrm{~\textrm~{~~}~}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{mV}}$ for the control, PEAI, and GRBP samples. By comparing to the HTL/perovskite stacks, the corresponding voltage losses at the perovskite/ETL interfaces are 114, 90, and $78~\mathrm{mV}_{\cdot}$ , indicating that the major defect-passivation behaviors occur at the perovskite/ETL interfaces. The ETL/control perovskite/HTL stack shows a low QFLS value owing to the serious nonradiative recombination at the perovskite/ETL interface. For the GRBP samples, the formed 2D perovskite can effectively reduce the nonradiative recombination at perovskite/ ETL interface which outperforms the PEAI samples that cannot form 2D perovskites on the film surface after posttreatment of single PEAI additive. The ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS) spectrum and calculated band alignment of the resulting cells are shown in Figure S10 (Supporting Information), the conduction band minimum (CBM) of GRBP films was closer to $\mathrm{C}_{60}$ than PEAI films, which resulted in more efficient charge transfer at the perovskite interface and the $\mathrm{C}_{60}$ electron transport layer. This may induce the GRBP has a better effect than PEAI process on reducing nonradiative recombination losses at perovskite/ETL interfaces. The $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ values estimated from the PLQY measurements are in good agreement with the values obtained from the $J{-}V$ results. The above results suggest that posttreatment with mixed MASCN and PEAI could synergistically reduce the nonradiative recombination losses in the bulk and at both the HTL/perovskite and perovskite/ETL interfaces.
To further assess the defect-passivation capabilities of posttreatment processes, we performed time-resolved photoluminescence (PL) measurements on the control, PEAI, and GRBP perovskite films (Figure  4c,d). The samples were prepared on bare glass substrates and were excited from both the perovskite surface and substrate sides. The wavelength of the excitation source is $405\mathrm{nm}$ , which is mainly absorbed at the illumination surface (penetration depth of ${\widetilde{\mathbf{\Gamma}}}{\approx}100~\mathrm{nm}$ in perovskite film).[34] For the front/back side cases, the PL decay lifetimes of the GRBP film $\mathrm{;23.7~ns/420.2~ns)}$ are longer than that of the PEAI film $(266.8\mathrm{ns}/212.0\mathrm{ns})$ ) and control film (186.2 ns/204.5 ns). The PL decay results indicate that both PEAI and mixed MASCN/PEAI can reduce the nonradiative recombination on the top surface of perovskite films, while the mixed additive can additionally passivate the buried interface.
The passivation mechanisms of PEAI and GRBP posttreatments are illustrated in Figure S11 (Supporting Information). For the PEAI case, the posttreatment of wide-bandgap perovskite film cannot form 2D perovskites on the top film surface, and the surface passivation are realized mainly on the top surface via the anchoring of long-chain PEA ligands. For the GRBP case, defect passivation is enabled at the GBs and the top/bottom bifacial interfaces between charge transport layer and perovskite absorber. To compare the photostability of perovskite films, we carried out PL measurements under continuous laser illumination in $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ environment (Figure S12, Supporting Information). Under 10-sun equivalent illumination for
$10~\mathrm{min}$ , GRBP films exhibited the lowest peak shift, indicating an improved photostability after the posttreatment of mixed MASCN and PEAI. Previous studies suggest that light-induced phase segregation in wide-bandgap perovskites mainly suffers from ion migration of halides and occurs easily especially at the GBs.[35] The GRBP strategy enlarge the grain size to reduce GBs, and the formed 2D perovskites at GBs and film surfaces can also restrict the ion migration within the perovskite film.
We then implemented the GRBP wide-bandgap perovskite in the 4-T perovskite/silicon tandem solar cells as schematically shown in Figure 5a, with the cross-sectional SEM image of semi-transparent WBG perovskite devices presented in Figure S13 (Supporting Information). The detailed measuring protocols are discussed in Note S2 and Figure S14 (Supporting Information). The perovskite top cell has a device structure of $\mathrm{MgF}_{2}$ /glass/ITO/SAM/perovskite/ $\mathrm{\Delta^{\prime}C_{60}/S n O_{2}/I T O/M g F_{2}}$ . A total of $140\ \mathrm{nm\MgF}_{2}$ film was coated on both sides of the device as antireflective coating, enhancing the light transmission between 800 and $1100\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Figure S15, Supporting Information). The bottom cell was SHJ solar cell (HIT). To maximize the current output of the silicon bottom cells, a $\mathrm{MgF}_{2}$ antireflection coating was also applied on the top surface of silicon solar cells, delivering an increased integrated current density of $18.2\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ for the bottom solar cells (Figure S16, Supporting Information; the value is $16.2\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ for the device without $\mathrm{MgF}_{2})$ ).
## 3. Conclusion
We report a grain regrowth and bifacial passivation strategy to reduce recombination losses at the grain boundaries and perovskite/charge transport layer interfaces simultaneously in WBG perovskite. In this process, MASCN and PEAI mixture solution is introduced for posttreatment. MASCN induces the regrowth of perovskite grains and simultaneously facilitates the penetration of PEAI into the HTL/perovskite bottom interface. By this strategy, the bulk and interfacial nonradiative recombination losses are reduced, and the open-circuit voltage in solar cells considerably increase. The PCEs of $21.9\%$ and $19.9\%$ for the $1.65\mathrm{-eV}$ bandgap opaque and semitransparent perovskite solar cells, respectively. We further fabricate perovskite/silicon 4-terminal tandem cells with an impressive PCEs of $29.8\%$ for $0.09\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}$ and $28.5\%$ for $1\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ devices. Furthermore, the semitransparent perovskite devices are stable under illumination, the encapsulated semitransparent perovskite solar cells retain their initial efficiency following $500\mathrm{~h~}$ of operation under onesun illumination in ambient conditions. This work provides a new strategy for reducing nonradiative recombination within the perovskite absorber and interfacial nonradiative recombination at the perovskite/charge extraction layer simultaneously. This strategy effectively improves the efficiency and stability of WBG perovskites and perovskite/silicon tandem solar cells, and accelerate the commercialization of perovskite–silicon photovoltaic technology.
## 4. Experimental Section
Materials: All materials were used as received without further purification. The organic halide salts (FAI, MABr, and CsI with purity of $>99\%)$ were purchased from GreatCell Solar Materials. Me-2PACZ and MeO-2PACZ $(>98\%$ purity) were purchased from TCI. $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ $(99.999\%)$ and $\mathsf{P b B r}_{2}$ $(99.999\%)$ were purchased from TCI. DMF $(99.8\%$ anhydrous), DMSO $(99.9\%$ anhydrous) and chlorobenzene $(99.8\%$ anhydrous) were purchased from Sigma–Aldrich. The ${\mathsf C}_{60}$ was purchased from Nano-C. BCP $(>99\%$ sublimed) was purchased from Xi'an Polymer Light Technology.
Precursor Solution Preparation: The $\mathsf{l}.4\mathrm{~}\mathsf{m}$ perovskite $\mathsf{F A}_{0.8}\mathsf{C s}_{0.15}\mathsf{M A}_{0.05}$ $\mathsf{P b}(\mathsf{I}_{0.82}\mathsf{B r}_{0.78})_{3}$ solution was formulated by mixing CsI, FAI, $\mathsf{P b B r}_{2}.$ , MABr, $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ with corresponding stoichiometry ratio dissolved in mixed solvents of DMF and DMSO with a volume ratio of 4:1.
Posttreatment: PEAI solution $(2\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ in IPA) and mixture solution $(2\ m g\ m L^{-1}$ PEAI and $1\mathrm{\mg\mL^{-1}}$ MASCN in IPA) were deposited on perovskite film for PEAI and GRBP process, respectively. An amount of $\mathord{\mathrm{~\textrm~{~~}~}}\mathord{\left.\mathrm{~\right.~}}50\mathrm{~\upmuL}$ solution was dropped onto the perovskite film for $5\ s$ and followed with the spin-coating procedure: 4000 r.p.m. for $20~\mathsf{s}$ with an acceleration of 4000 r.p.m.s. Then the as-deposited film was annealed at $100^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for 5  min. After cooling to room temperature, the annealed films were washed by $700~\upmu\upiota$ IPA with the same spin coating procedure mentioned above, then the washed film was transferred to a $100^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ hot plate and dried for $3\mathrm{\min}$ .
Top-Illuminated Semitransparent Cells Fabrication: A p-i-n device structure was employed. The prepatterned indium-doped tin oxide glass was sequentially cleaned via sonication in acetone and isopropanol followed by UV-ozone for 15 min. HTL was formed by spinning ${\mathsf{100~}}\upmu\mathsf{L}$ 1 mmol Me-2PACZ and MEO-2PACZ mixed solution with a molar ratio of 3:1 on an ITO substrate at 4000 r.p.m. with an acceleration of 4000 r.p.m. s for $20\thinspace s$ and postannealed at $100^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for 5 min. Then ${\mathsf{100}}\upmu\mathsf{L}$ IPA solution was dropped onto the annealed film to wash out the residual SAM on the substrate with a dynamically spin-casting process at 4000 r.p.m. for $\rceil0\ s$ . Then the washed film was transferred to a $100^{\circ}\mathsf C$ hot plate and dried for 3  min. For top-illuminated semitransparent devices, $20\ n m$ of ${\mathsf C}_{60},$ , and $20\ \mathsf{n m}$ of $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ were deposited by ALD, $80~\mathsf{n m}$ of ITO was deposited by a magnetron sputtering method and $200\ n m$ of $\mathsf{A g}$ was sequentially deposited by thermal evaporation.
Characterization: The scanning electron microscopy images were obtained using a Hitachi S8100 microscope with an accelerating voltage of $2\mathsf{k V}.$ The grazing-incidence $\mathsf{X}$ -ray scattering (GI-XRD) data were recorded using a Xeuss 3.0 system. The photon energy was $9.24~\mathrm{keV}$ (wavelength $=1.3474\mathring\mathsf{A})$ , and the incident angles are $0.05^{\circ}$ and $7^{\circ}$ . The diffracted X-rays were collected by an Eiger $2{\textsf{R}}{\textsf{I M}}$ detector at a distance of $2000\ \mathsf{m m}$ . XPS analysis was carried out using the Thermo Scientific Al K-alpha XPS system with energy steps of 0.1 eV. The powder XRD spectra ( $\theta{-}2\theta$ scans) were taken on a D8 powder X-ray diffraction system (PANalytical Inc.) with $\mathsf{C u}\ \mathsf{K}\alpha$ $(\lambda=1.{\overset{\cdot}{5}}478\ {\overset{\circ}{\ A}}$ ) as the X-ray source. PL signal was analyzed by a Horiba Jobin Yvon system. Absorption spectra were recorded using an Agilent Technologies Cary Series UV–vis–NIR Spectrophotometer.
